ough I actually  think that method comparisons still  provide a lot of important information  to related to how your test is  performing because knowing how your test  correlates with other methods or other  assays will be very important and in  guiding clinicians and helping them  understand results that they might see  on one method versus another  what do we say what can we say about  clinical sensitivity and specificity I  think while again this is important to  do it can be very challenging especially  when you have access to limited samples  especially when you have a diagnosis  it's relatively rare  this can be very time consuming and very  labor intensive to do chart review to  collect samples from patients with known  diagnoses  very challenging but similar to Method  comparisons if it's if it's something  you're able to do I think it does  provide a lot of valuable information  regarding how this method or how your  method is going to perform from a  clinical diagnostic perspective  so that's the end of my presentation  I'll just summarize a few points here  um we know Auto antibodies are very  heterogeneous and that can lead to a lot  of challenges for The Clinical  Laboratory  we know there are regulatory  requirements that we have to meet for  assay verification  but how we approach these for auto  antibody serology test is not always  clear  and I'll leave you with this last point  and this is something that I like to do  when I'm designing a verification design  and verification experiments for auto  antibody testing I ask myself two  questions first the first question is  how do I fulfill the regulatory  requirements this is something that we  need to be able to do but beyond that I  want to ask the question really what do  I need to know about this assay  performance in order to make sure that I  have both an analytically and clinically  robust assay and oftentimes we need to  go beyond those regulatory requirements  and in order to satisfy ourselve